<div class="article-body">
    <div class="callout">
      <h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2>
      <ol class="stop-steps">
        <li><strong>Cannot hang yet:</strong> start with feet flat on the floor or a box, then toes touching, then short holds with a box beneath you.</li>
        <li><strong>First target:</strong> 3 sets of 15 seconds with as much help as you need. Rest 90 to 120 seconds between sets.</li>
        <li><strong>Grip:</strong> overhand at shoulder width with thumbs wrapped and arms fully straight.</li>
        <li><strong>Weekly plan:</strong> 3 sessions on non-consecutive days. Add about 5 seconds to your target once every set is clean. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</li>
        <li><strong>Stop and step down</strong> for sharp pain, numbness, light-headedness or a pinching shoulder.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>Starting From Zero</h2>
      <p>A first dead hang of a few seconds is a normal starting point for a beginner, and first-attempt hold times vary widely. Some people cannot hang at all yet. That is not a sign of weakness. Grip endurance usually improves with regular practice.</p>

      <p>Your pace depends on bodyweight and starting strength. Three sessions a week of 5 to 10 minutes each is a common plan. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription. Read the <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">how to dead hang guide</a> for the basic movement pattern before starting.</p>

      <p>Regression exercises build the grip foundation needed for a full dead hang. Start with these if you cannot hold the bar for 5 seconds yet.</p>

      <h2 id="cant-hang-yet">Can't Hang Yet? Start With This Ladder</h2>

      <p>If you cannot hang from the bar yet, start on the lowest rung and move up one rung at a time. Each rung removes a little support. The notes on when to move up are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

      <div class="visual-block">
        <div class="dg-box"><div class="dg-box-head"><span>Starting ladder</span><span>Four rungs</span></div>
<ol class="sl-steps" aria-label="Starting ladder from a supported hang to Level 1">
<li><div class="sl-fig">${hangFigureSvg({ pose: 'supported', hl: 'legs' })}</div><div class="sl-text"><p class="sl-name"><span class="sl-n">Rung 1</span> Supported hang</p><p><span class="sl-k">What it looks like</span>Feet flat on the floor or a box, hands on a low bar. Your legs carry most of your weight and the bar takes a small share.</p><p><span class="sl-k">Ready for the next rung when</span>You finish your planned sets with steady form and no pain, and shifting a little more weight onto the bar feels manageable.</p></div></li>
<li><div class="sl-fig">${hangFigureSvg({ pose: 'supported', assist: 'toes', hl: 'legs' })}</div><div class="sl-text"><p class="sl-name"><span class="sl-n">Rung 2</span> Feet-assisted hang</p><p><span class="sl-k">What it looks like</span>Toes touching the floor or a box, or one or both feet in a loop band. The bar carries most of your weight and the floor or band takes the rest. Record the assist you used.</p><p><span class="sl-k">Ready for the next rung when</span>You finish every set with only light toe contact or a thinner band, and the hold feels steady.</p></div></li>
<li><div class="sl-fig">${hangFigureSvg({ pose: 'dead-hang', box: true, hl: 'arms' })}</div><div class="sl-text"><p class="sl-name"><span class="sl-n">Rung 3</span> Short controlled holds</p><p><span class="sl-k">What it looks like</span>Feet off the ground, arms straight, for a few seconds at a time. A box or step sits beneath you. Step down under control before your grip gives out.</p><p><span class="sl-k">Ready for the next rung when</span>You finish every set of short holds cleanly and want to lengthen them toward 15 seconds.</p></div></li>
<li><div class="sl-fig">${hangFigureSvg({ pose: 'dead-hang', sets: 3, hl: 'grip' })}</div><div class="sl-text"><p class="sl-name"><span class="sl-n">Rung 4</span> Level 1 hang</p><p><span class="sl-k">What it looks like</span>Three sets of 15 seconds, with as much assistance as you need to reach 15. Rest 90 seconds between sets.</p><p><span class="sl-k">Ready for the next rung when</span>You hold 15 seconds across all 3 sets with minimal assistance. Level 2 starts there; see the <a href="/deadhang-progressions/">dead hang progressions</a>.</p></div></li>
</ol></div>
        ${demoLoopHtml('feet-to-hang', { caption: 'Rung 1 to rung 3: stepping off the box and hanging with straight arms.' })}
        <p class="caption">Schematic only. Stay on a rung for as long as you need.</p>
      </div>

      <p>For feet-off holds, one common pattern is 3 sets of 5 seconds, then adding about 5 seconds once every set is clean. Never hang to failure. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
      <p>Stop and step down if you notice sharp pain, numbness, light-headedness or a pinching shoulder. The <a href="/deadhang-safety/#stop-and-step-down">stop rules</a> list each sign and what to do.</p>

      <h2>Why Can't I Dead Hang for More Than 5 Seconds?</h2>
      <p>Most beginners stop at a few seconds because the fingers and forearms tire first, and a heavier bodyweight makes the same hold harder. Grip endurance usually improves with regular practice. Work out which of these three applies to you.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Wrong rung:</strong> if you cannot reach 5 seconds, drop to the supported or feet-assisted rung above.</li>
        <li><strong>Grip burn:</strong> forearm burning is normal. Sharp pain, numbness or a pinching shoulder means stop and step down.</li>
        <li><strong>Too little rest:</strong> rest 90 to 120 seconds between sets and train on non-consecutive days.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Regression Exercises</h2>

      <p>These three exercises build toward a full dead hang. They add detail to the first rungs of the ladder above. Use the ladder to decide when to move on.</p>

      <h3>1. Feet-on-Ground Hang</h3>

      <p>Grip a low bar or set a pull-up bar at chest height. Keep your feet on the ground and gradually reduce foot contact so more of your weight hangs from the bar. Foot support takes part of your weight. Record the assist you used so tests stay comparable. Hold for 15 to 30 seconds.</p>

      <p>This exercise teaches your hands to grip under load without the fear of falling. Practice it a few times per session until the hold feels steady with little foot contact.</p>

      <h3>2. Band-Assisted Hang</h3>

      <p>Loop a resistance band over the bar and place one or both feet in it. The band takes part of your weight, and thicker bands take more. Record the band you used so tests stay comparable. Hang with fully extended arms for 10 to 20 seconds. Use progressively thinner bands as you get stronger.</p>

      <p>Band-assisted hangs replicate the dead hang position with reduced load. That makes them a close step before a full bodyweight dead hang. The same band idea carries over later to assisted reps in a <a href="/dead-hang-pull-up/">dead hang pull-up</a>.</p>

      <h3>3. Flexed-Arm Hang</h3>

      <p>A flexed-arm hang needs elbow-flexor strength and is harder than a hang for many untrained people. The hold builds arm strength alongside grip once you can already hold a chin-up position. Jump or step up to the bar with your chin above it (top of a chin-up position) and hold that position for about 15 seconds. Stop before your form slips.</p>

      <p>Your biceps and forearms share the load. Aim for 3 sets of 15 to 20 seconds before progressing. Schools use the same bent-arm hold as the <a href="/flexed-arm-hang/">flexed arm hang</a> test.</p>

      <h2>How Do You Do Your First Dead Hang?</h2>
      <p>A first dead hang takes seven cues, listed below. Stand beneath the bar. Reach up and grip it with both hands at shoulder width. Wrap your thumbs fully around the bar. Lift your feet off the ground and let your arms straighten completely.</p>

      <h3>Step-by-Step Form</h3>

      <ol>
        <li>Grip the bar with a double overhand grip at shoulder width</li>
        <li>Wrap thumbs around the bar (not a thumbless grip)</li>
        <li>Lift feet off the ground and let your body hang</li>
        <li>Straighten your arms fully with no bend at the elbows</li>
        <li>Pull shoulders slightly away from your ears</li>
        <li>Breathe slowly through your nose</li>
        <li>Look straight ahead or slightly downward</li>
      </ol>

      <p>Aim for 5-10 seconds on your first real dead hang. Anything above zero counts as progress. Read the full <a href="/deadhang-form/">dead hang form guide</a> for detailed technique cues.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Safety first:</strong> Hang from a bar you can reach without jumping. Jumping onto the bar risks losing grip on contact. Use a step or box to reach the bar safely.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>How Do You Build to a 30-Second Dead Hang?</h2>
      <p>A beginner builds to a 30-second dead hang by training 3 sessions a week and raising the target hold slowly. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days. Add a session only if you stay pain free. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week. Each session takes less than 10 minutes.</p>

      <h3>Weekly Structure</h3>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Sessions per week:</strong> 3 on non-consecutive days (for example Monday, Wednesday, Friday)</li>
        <li><strong>Sets per session:</strong> 3</li>
        <li><strong>Rest between sets:</strong> 90-120 seconds</li>
        <li><strong>Progression:</strong> Add about 5 seconds once every set is clean, roughly weekly</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Sample 4-Week Progression</h3>

      <p>The 4-week table is an example of how targets can rise, not a norm.</p>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Week</th>
            <th scope="col">Target Per Set</th>
            <th scope="col">Sessions</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Week 1</strong></td>
            <td>5-10 seconds</td>
            <td>3</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Week 2</strong></td>
            <td>10-15 seconds</td>
            <td>3</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Week 3</strong></td>
            <td>15-22 seconds</td>
            <td>3</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Week 4</strong></td>
            <td>22-30 seconds</td>
            <td>3</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>

      <p>The <a href="/4-week-program/">4-week beginner dead hang plan</a> lays these weeks out as a worked example with a test day.</p>

      <h2>Common Beginner Challenges</h2>
      <p>The four challenges beginners meet most often are a weak grip, shoulder discomfort, skin soreness and fear of height. Each has a simple fix, and sharp pain is the one sign to stop for.</p>
<h3>Weak Grip</h3>

      <p>Grip strength is the primary limiter for beginners. Your fingers and forearms fatigue before the rest of your body. Grip endurance usually improves with regular practice. Chalk helps if sweat, not fatigue, ends your hang. It does not replace training volume.</p>

      <h3>Shoulder Discomfort</h3>

      <p>Mild stretching sensations in the shoulders are normal during dead hangs. Sharp or stabbing pain is not. Stop the hang if you feel sharp pain. Check the <a href="/deadhang-form/">form guide</a> to ensure your shoulders are packed correctly. See a physiotherapist if pain persists across multiple sessions.</p>

      <h3>Calluses and Skin Pain</h3>

      <p>Calluses commonly form at the base of the fingers with regular hanging. File calluses smooth to reduce tearing if they become thick. Grip the bar in the fingers rather than the palm to reduce callus buildup.</p>

      <h3>Fear of Height</h3>

      <p>Pull-up bars feel higher than they look. Start with a low bar where your feet nearly touch the ground. Build confidence with short hangs before moving to a full-height bar. Placing a mat or foam beneath you reduces the psychological barrier.</p>

      <h2>Equipment for Beginners</h2>

      <p>Minimal equipment gets you started. Upgrade as your training progresses.</p>

      <h3>Essential: A Pull-Up Bar</h3>

      <p>A <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorframe pull-up bar</a> installs in seconds. Check the rated load before you buy. Prices vary.</p>

      <h3>Recommended: Chalk</h3>

      <p>Gym chalk dries the skin and increases friction between skin and bar. Use it if sweat, not fatigue, ends your hang. Sweaty hands can make you drop early.</p>

      <h3>Optional: Comfortable Grip Aids</h3>

      <p>Foam bar padding or grip wraps may reduce hand pain in the first weeks. Remove them as calluses develop.</p>

      <h2>When You Are No Longer a Beginner</h2>

      <p>This site treats a 30-second dead hang with clean form as the point where the beginner plan ends and the intermediate plan starts. It is a training band and not a published standard.</p>

      <h3>Signs You Are Ready to Progress</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>30-second overhand dead hang completed 3 times in one session</li>
        <li>No shoulder, elbow, or wrist pain during or after hanging</li>
        <li>Calluses developed and skin no longer tears</li>
        <li>Breathing stays controlled throughout each hang</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Move to the <a href="/intermediate-deadhang/">intermediate dead hang training guide</a> for grip variation work and longer holds. The training programs page helps you pick between the 4-week plan (if you need more structure) and the 8-week plan framework (once you can hold a comfortable 20 to 30 seconds).</p>

      <div class="cta-box">
        <h3>Ready for a Structured Plan?</h3>
        <p>The 4-week plan lays out three sessions a week with sets, hold targets and a test day.</p>
        <a href="/4-week-program/" class="cta-btn">Open the 4-Week Plan</a>
      </div>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>How long should a beginner dead hang for?</h3>
        <p>Aim for 5 to 10 seconds on your first real dead hang, and count anything above zero as progress. Then build to 3 sets of 15 seconds with as much help as you need. Add about 5 seconds once every set is clean. This is common training practice and not a tested prescription.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Why can't I dead hang for more than 5 seconds?</h3>
        <p>Most beginners stop early because the fingers and forearms tire first, and a heavier bodyweight makes the hold harder. Drop to a feet-supported or band-assisted hang, rest 90 to 120 seconds between sets, and add about 5 seconds only once every set is clean. Stop for sharp pain.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>What if I cannot hang from the bar at all?</h3>
        <p>Start on the lowest rung: feet flat on the floor or a box with your hands on a low bar. Then move to toes touching or a loop band. Each rung removes a little support. Move up only when you finish every set with steady form and no pain.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Is dead hanging safe for a beginner with loose shoulders?</h3>
        <p>Ask a physical therapist or doctor first if you have shoulder hypermobility, instability or past dislocations. Two physiotherapy academics advise ten seconds or fewer to start if you are cleared to hang. No trial has tested dead hangs in hypermobile people. Stop and step down for sharp pain.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>How often should a beginner dead hang?</h3>
        <p>Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days, for example Monday, Wednesday and Friday. Add a session only if you stay pain free, and leave at least one rest day between hard sessions. Each session takes less than 10 minutes. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="faq">
        <h3>Why do my hands hurt when I dead hang?</h3>
        <p>Skin soreness and calluses at the base of the fingers are common with regular hanging. Grip the bar in the fingers rather than the palm, and file thick calluses smooth so they do not tear. Chalk helps if sweat ends your hang. Sharp pain or numbness is a reason to stop.</p>
      </div>

